That's true.  That makes it a very bad idea, then, because on multi-user
systems levels 4 through 10 would most likely stay bones; look at the
number of deaths on NAO and how many of them come early on? 

In vanilla, this has the effect of granting a cursed cloak of magic
resistance to a high number of players (since wizards are so common),
and Excalibur with a +3 small shield to a similarly high number (since 
lawful valkyries are so common).  Tinning kits from Arkies,... the list
is not particularly small.  Most classes have a piece or two of starting
equipment that another class would be _delighted_ to find, even cursed.

Even without those two items, I think overfrequent early bones would
sufficiently disrupt the early-game balance in favor of the player that 
it ends up being a bad idea.

> was driving at; currently on a multi-user system, if you get bones
> today, nobody else will get bones at that level until someone else
> dies on that level. Hmm, but if it's a popular level to die, then the
> time that slot stays empty is small.

Correct.

> This might be a bad thing, at least from a "sporky" point of view.

Increasing the frequency of bones is less bad from a Spork point of view
because the "uber items" aren't _the_ means to an end  anymore.  Magic
resistance is valuable, as is reflection, but neither of these
contribute as much to "The thinking portion of this game is over" as they
do in vanilla.

This still doesn't mean I like it, especially from the point of view of
a public server.  It would be a lot better for a single-user setup, but
it would also mean that you'd "eventually" get your equipment back as
long as you died at a deep enough level -- because after a certain
point, the save chance of bones is fairly high, and with no other
restrictions on bones, at least one of your games would collect it.
That removes a large part of the penalty for dying.

